Article 37

To provide Protection as referred to in Article 35, the Regional Government may undertake efforts to:
a. provide services and handling as quickly as possible to witnesses and/or Victims;
b. provide convenience, comfort, safety, and free of charge for witnesses and/or Victims;
c. maintain the confidentiality of witnesses and/or Victims;
d. guarantee justice and legal certainty for witnesses and/or Victims; e. provision of special service rooms at Police Offices;
f. provision of apparatus, health workers, social workers, and spiritual counselors; g. creation and development of cooperation systems and mechanisms for service programs involving parties that are easily accessible to Victims; h. provide Protection for companions, witnesses, families, and friends of Victims;
i. provide Protection for female workers/laborers in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Article 12

1. Every woman has the right to: a. live and maintain life and improve her standard of living;
b. fulfill her basic needs to grow and develop properly, have a family and continue offspring;
c. develop her personality, to obtain education, and improve her quality of life to become a faithful, pious, responsible, noble, happy, and prosperous human being in accordance with human rights;
d. obtain justice, security, and freedom to express opinions without discrimination;
e. be free from slavery or enslavement and threats;
f. obtain protection of her person, family, honor, dignity and property rights;
g. obtain welfare and a decent life;
h. participate in politics;
i. perform legal acts;
j. freely choose a partner in her marriage. k. choose the work she likes and is also entitled to fair employment conditions; and l. receive fair wages in accordance with her work that is comparable and commensurate with human dignity (2) The right to participate in politics as referred to in paragraph (1) letter h is, women have the right to: a. vote and/or be elected in general elections, regional head elections, village head elections and/or other political office elections based on equal rights through direct, general, free, secret voting; and b. be appointed in every government position.

4.1.text:
Article 29

1. The Regional Government is obliged and responsible to carry out efforts to prevent the occurrence of Violence against Women in the form of: a. collecting data and information about Victims of violence and Laws and Regulations;
b. conducting education on anti-Violence against Women values;
c. conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to the implementation of Protection for Victims of violence; d. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of Protection of Victims of violence; and e. preparing reports on the implementation of Protection of women Victims of violence.
2. To anticipate the occurrence of acts of Violence against Women, the Regional Government is obliged to provide and organize services for victims in the form of: a. establishing and facilitating the implementation of integrated service institutions for Victims by involving community elements; and b. encouraging community awareness of the importance of Protection for Victims.
3. The Regional Government in carrying out obligations as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) must pay attention to the rights and obligations of parents, guardians or other persons who are legally responsible for the victim.
4. To prevent the occurrence of acts of Violence against Women, the Regional Government makes efforts as follows: a. conducting counseling and socialization regarding matters relating to Violence against Women;
b. conducting socialization of women's rights;
c. seeking to improve education for women;
d. opening skills courses for women to support the economic needs of the family;
e. creating employment opportunities for women;
f. providing counseling to immediately report to local authorities if Violence against Women occurs;
g. empowering Social Community Organizations and women's organizations to increase women's knowledge and insight;
h. establishing Women's Protection Complaint Posts; and i. forming work networks in violence prevention efforts.

Article 8

Promotive efforts as referred to in Article 7 are as follows: a. strengthening coordination mechanisms and service unit networks in efforts to handle violence cases;
b. providing Communication, Information and Education materials related to prevention and handling of violence; and c. organizing socialization, advocacy and social campaigns in the context of prevention and handling of violence.

Article 9

Preventive efforts as referred to in Article 7 are as follows: a. conducting legal awareness counseling for the community, especially women and children;
b. conducting massive and sustainable movements involving the community in prevention and handling of violence actions;
c. instilling character values, morals, and family resilience; and d. involving the role and participation of the community in women empowerment and child protection.

Article 36

1. Forms of Protection against Violence against Women include: a. Medical protection;
b. Legal protection;
c. Medico-legal protection (forensic medicine);
d. Economic protection; ande. Psychological protection.
2. The form of medical protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a, is treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of Victims carried out by medical personnel and paramedics.
3. The form of legal protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, is services and assistance to help Victims in undergoing legal and judicial processes.
4. The form of medico-legal protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c, is medical services for the purposes of evidence in the field of law.
5. The form of economic protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d, is services for skills and to provide economic access so that victims of violence can be independent.
6. The form of psychological protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter e is psychological support so that victims have self-confidence, strength and independence in problem solving, assistance in order to restore the traumatic condition of Victims including the provision of safe houses to protect victims from various threats and intimidation for victims and provide.

Article 6

In order to realize the objectives as referred to in Article 3 letter b, efforts shall be made through:
a. establishment, development, and capacity strengthening of women and children protection institutions including complaint service units for violence against women and children, as well as legal aid services;
b. improvement of the quality of human resources management;
c. capacity strengthening of PUG and children institutions; and d. strengthening and development of gender and children data systems.

Article 10

Curative efforts as referred to in Article 7 are as follows: a. optimizing technical service units related to complaints of violence against women and children;
b. providing adequate facilities and infrastructure for handling health rehabilitation, social rehabilitation, repatriation, social reintegration, and legal aid; and c. conducting handling for victims of crime and violence quickly, precisely and accurately by law enforcement officers.

Article 13

Every woman victim of violence has:
a. the right to be respected for her dignity as a human being; b. the right to recovery of health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by the victim;
c. the right to determine her own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to compensation;
g. the right to social rehabilitation;
h. the right to complaint handling;
i. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilities in the judicial process; and j. the right to assistance.

Article 17

The Regent in implementing policies, programs, and activities for women protection shall make efforts to:
a. coordinate the implementation of policies, programs, and activities;
b. protect women among Regional Apparatus;
c. cooperate with Regencies/Cities within one Province, and cooperation between Regencies/Cities in other Provinces in the implementation of policies, programs, and activities for women protection in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
d. strengthen the institutional capacity of PUG for the implementation of policies, programs and activities for women protection;
e. facilitate women protection services;
f. provide women protection services;
g. implement affirmative action for women protection; and h. prepare a women protection data system.

Article 4

The scope of this Regional Regulation includes:
a. rights and obligations of women;
b. obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government;
c. protection of women;
d. empowerment of women;
e. protection of women victims of violence;
f. community participation and involvement;
g. monitoring, evaluation and reporting; andh. funding.

